Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Parcel 28 Changes Hands Six Times
- Story & Context: At 134 BPM, Ratri designs a six-note angklung hook distributed across six performers from far left to center-right. Mateus uses only kick, clap, and one short tom answer during the first relay so the spatial pitch travel remains obvious. A Dispatch Gap appears between notes four and five; Dimas inserts one woodblock hit into the vacancy. The sixth note lands back on the original pitch class but from a different player, creating the first Double Address illusion. Nádia waits until the complete relay ends before striking one dry suspended-second Wurlitzer shell. João enters with a short bass-trombone fifth only after the second full cycle.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Postal parcel card marked 28 with six stamped handoff boxes connected by arrows, bamboo angklung frames positioned like route markers across a warehouse floor plan, one red RETURN stamp partly visible. Sparse lyric fragments: “parcel twenty-eight,” “six hands,” “pass it right,” “not here yet.”

Track 2: Return to Sender from Aisle C
- Story & Context: At 138 BPM, the track foregrounds Return Receipt. A seven-note melody begins with the leftmost angklung player, crosses four other positions, doubles one central pitch using Double Address, then returns to the original performer for the final note. Mateus withholds the sub and main clap until that last return occurs. When the starting player finally sounds, kick, clap, sub, and prepared electric piano confirm the cadence together for a single beat. Midway through, the route is reversed so the same melody travels right-to-left. Dimas uses short reco-reco scrapes during Dispatch Gaps, making the empty spaces feel active without replacing the missing bamboo notes.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Long sorting aisle labeled C with red directional arrows leaving one mail slot and curving back to the same slot, paired angklung silhouettes marking a duplicate address in the center. Sparse lyric fragments: “aisle C,” “wrong address,” “send it back,” “return received.”

Track 3: Last Route Card Has No Solo Line
- Story & Context: The closer runs at 126 BPM and begins with no sub and almost no hats. Ratri gives twelve angklung players one or two notes each, constructing the longest relay on the EP. No performer’s card contains enough information to reconstruct the full melody alone. Nádia plays only three electric-piano chords in the entire first half. João uses low breath-heavy trombone pedals during the largest Dispatch Gaps. As the piece develops, Mateus removes more electronic percussion while the relay grows faster, forcing the acoustic network to carry both melody and perceived subdivision. In the final minute, every player sounds one note in rapid sequence from left to right, then the line reverses back toward the starting position. The expected final Return Receipt is omitted. The last player holds the bamboo frame still, and the track ends on room tone rather than a resolving note.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Final route card showing twelve separate numbered boxes but no complete melody staff, empty solo column crossed out in red pencil, long warehouse aisle disappearing into darkness. Sparse lyric fragments: “no solo line,” “one note each,” “last route,” “do not return.”

